A||||7||Joe Trainer||Oil Additives||||5||
Z||000000||Joe Trainer||01-31-2000||11:38 PM||||Don't know if this is a proper question for this forum but, are any of the oil additives any good for your Fiero engines?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||kong||02-01-2000||12:11 AM||||This is going to sound like a commercial but, I had a 77 Cougar that I force fit a 460 into. balanced 0 decked .30 over, 9.8 c.r. comp cam 556/lift 292/dur. 750 holley dbl pumper. I figured I would give duralube a try. Anyway  I got in a hurry one day and I didn't tighten the oil pan drain plug.<BR>     About 3 days later I was rompin and stompin the monster about 2 miles from a friends garage when it started making all kinds of nasty noises, I check ed the gauges and sure enough 0 oil pressure. I figured I had already fried it and drove it to my friends garage rattling and banging all the way.<BR> I killed it and coasted the last block. We got a new plug,oil,and filter on it and fired it back up and the oil pump picked up and pumped up the lifters and it quieted down and ran as good as ever. This happens five years ago. I finally sold it to a guy here in town, and its running good today. I put it in my wifes t-bird my fiero, engine and trans, in both my bikes, and even in my lawn mower (10 yrs old) and all of them run great. <BR> This will probably start a debate about additives but this made a believer out of me.<BR>     ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Patrick||02-01-2000||12:39 AM||||<BR>We've covered oil additives at least a couple times here (and we have a really good <B>Search</B> function... hint, hint). I have my own story regarding <B>Dura-Lube</B> in this thread:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/000105.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/000105.html</A> <P>and here's a thread from two weeks ago:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001989.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001989.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||olympic||02-01-2000||07:42 PM||||   Synthetic oils also bond to metal surfaces and provide protection at start-up.  The thing with oil additives is that you have no idea what your putting in your engine. You're takeing the word of some guy on an infomercial for what it will do for your engine.  BTW, when they say that they drove the car across country with "no oil" they're not exactly lying.  If you look at the microscopic print at the bottom of your TV it says that there is no motor oil in the engine but they put in 24 oz of duralube or whatever.  Pretty sneaky.  Anyways, Slick 50 is nothing more than powdered Teflon in 50 weight oil.  Many of the company's who make these additives have been forced to remove bogus claims from their advertising.   I say spend your money on a good synthetic and forget about these additives. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||02-02-2000||08:30 PM||||Consumer Reports did a test on these additives ( Dura Lude, and Slick-50 ) and found that they were all just a scam.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87PegasusGT||02-04-2000||04:04 PM||||Oympic and Formula88 are 100% correct. I used to sell them. 99% of the additives at the store do more harm than good. Water "removing" additives merely include alcohol which is hard on your fuel lines. The Chevron w/Techron takes the acid at the bottom of your fuel tank and chrystalizes it thus lining your injectors. I have tested very many and the only decent store brand that actually doesn't kill anything is Redline. Very spendy though. A gentleman named Gary Lynch created his own line of products for use with his oval racer, but you can't buy them in stores. Being a former distributor, I know which shops carry them. Lynch Proformance Products are based out of Redmond, Or , so I don't know how available they are at your location, but I will vouch on the quality of these products having done numerous tests and comparisons. They are only available through certain shops and dealerships.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula||02-04-2000||09:12 PM||||the water removing things only work if you have "water" in your gas, I used that stuff before and was suprised that it did actually work.<P>btw, I recommend anything with dialkyldithiophosphate in it!!!!<P>I even spread it on my mirrors.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||02-05-2000||02:56 PM||||I usually only use a gas dryer 2-3 times a year. usually you won't get to much water in a gas tank unless there's a problem or you get gas from a bad station tank. If you get a bad station tank you will likely need to sophone out the watter. (( a 1/4" tube with a weighted end works good for this.)) Then once you do that a couple tanks with drygas will get the last of it. beats the hell out of dropping gastanks.<P>Periodic use of such a product usually won't hurt a thing, and in the case of gas dryers will prevent a line or injector from ice. You don't need to use it all the time like many people do. no gas aditive product will fix a really dirty system without likely causing some other problem like all the dirt coming loose at once and pluging jets and injectors.<P>As for oil aditives I haven't seen one yet that can really proove it does anything. Anecdotal evidence is about all I've ever heard. So far everytime Consumer Reports and other testing centers have tryed to duplicate many of these so called "indepandant" tests and the run w/o oil thing they have failed in exactly the same way as off the shelf oil. At least one of the infomercial pushers is being investigated by the FTC because of these "tests" they claim to have run.<P>DuPont even tried to stop sales of Teflon and other PTFE resin to these clowns. DuPont didn't want their trademarked product used in a way even they don't believe works. A number of tests have shown much of the PTFE ends up in the oil filter on the first pass. In addition to not doing anything for the motor it is now plugging the filter. Some makers claim they can grind PTFE fine enough to get past the filter. Even DuPont doesn't believe them. One would think the maker of Teflon knows how small it can be ground up. It's likely the've tried. ((I've seen the majority of the DuPont "Experimental Station" in Wilmington DE. If it can be done with a DuPont product they will try it. They will try it just for the hell of it some times. It's pretty rare when someone finds a property of a DuPont product before the Station does.))<P>Other additives are boosted packages of the things normally added to engine oil. Some of these chemicals can actually harm a motor when used above certain concentrations.<P>Yet others are god knows what and the makers won't tell you at all.||||reg||1
